.TH EVENT_LOOP_REMOVE 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
event_loop_remove \- deregister a selectable from an event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t event_loop_remove(event_loop loop, event_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Deregisters reg.  Safe to call from within a callback for the very
registration being removed (self-removal on error is a common pattern),
as well as from any other thread, including concurrently with an
in-flight dispatch for the same registration.  No further callback for
this registration is ever invoked after this call returns, even one
already collected (e.g. sitting queued for a dispatch worker thread with
num_reactor_threads > 1) but not yet actually started; this call itself,
however, does not wait for a dispatch that is already in flight at the
moment of the call to finish.  A caller that must not free whatever arg
points to while such a dispatch could still be running needs
handlers.on_removed (see event_loop_add(3)): it fires exactly once,
asynchronously, only once no dispatch of this registration and no other
event_loop call resolving it can still be in progress.  A caller
confident no dispatch can be in flight at the moment of removal (e.g.
self-removal from within that exact registration's own callback, or a
registration that was never dispatched to begin with) may still free arg
immediately without needing on_removed.

Does not close an fd or affect a queue's own lifetime; only the
event_loop's registration bookkeeping is released, mirroring
selectable_from_fd's existing "caller owns the fd" contract.

Safe to call more than once on the same reg, whether sequentially or
genuinely concurrently from different threads, and the reg handle
itself remains safe to pass to a subsequent event_loop_modify(),
event_loop_pause()/_resume(), event_loop_reg_generation(), or
event_loop_remove() call after removal: reg is resolved against its
own loop's registration table before anything is dereferenced, so an
already-removed or otherwise stale handle is always detected rather
than risking a use-after-free. The two repeat-call cases return
differently, though. A call made strictly after an earlier
event_loop_remove() on the same reg has already returned finds reg_h
no longer resolves at all (that earlier call already released reg's
own slot before returning) and gets ccol_invalid_args, the same
outcome event_loop_modify()/_pause()/_resume()/event_loop_reg_
generation() already give for an already-removed reg. A call that
genuinely races another event_loop_remove() on the same reg is
different: if its own resolve still succeeds (a narrow window, since
reg's slot has not yet been released by the other call), it observes
reg already marked removed and returns ccol_success as a no-op
instead.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  event_loop the registration belongs to.
reg   Registration to remove.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        Removed (or a genuinely concurrent racing call
                    against the same reg already removed it first;
                    see DESCRIPTION).
ccol_invalid_args   loop is EVENT_LOOP_INVALID or a stale/already-
                    destroyed handle, or reg is EVENT_REG_INVALID or
                    otherwise fails to resolve, including because an
                    earlier, already-returned event_loop_remove() call
                    already removed it.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from any thread, including concurrently with dispatch for
the same registration and from within that registration's own callback.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void on_readable(event_loop loop, ccol_selectable *sel, void *arg) {
    conn_ctx_t *ctx = (conn_ctx_t *)arg;
    char buf[512];
    ssize_t n = read(sel->fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
    if (n <= 0) {
        event_loop_remove(loop, ctx->reg);  /* self-removal, safe */
        close(sel->fd);
        free(ctx);
        return;
    }
    /* ... */
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR event_loop_add (3),
.BR event_loop_modify (3),
.BR event_loop_pause (3),
.BR event_loop_resume (3),
.BR event_loop_destroy (3)
